body:
Canvas / MCP Apps architecture triage decision

The earlier lead was independently checked against current repository state and the official MCP Apps extension. Correction: Action Server does not currently generate or serve a verified MCP App Canvas resource. The Canvas Vite root is a placeholder boundary; generic run-artifact serving does not establish ui:// resources or text/html;profile=mcp-app. Internal _meta.ui.resourceUri fixtures prove preservation, not a supported public authoring API.

Verified standard model:
- tool _meta.ui.resourceUri identifies a ui:// resource;
- the MCP server registers and serves text/html;profile=mcp-app through resources/read;
- an MCP Apps-capable host renders a sandboxed iframe and provides the JSON-RPC postMessage bridge;
- the UI reaches MCP tools through the host. No separate Canvas protocol, Python Canvas runtime, or standalone client is needed.

Issue ownership:
- #99: MCP App View resource/bridge/renderer and host conformance;
- #100: smallest public actions-core/actions.mcp tool-metadata and UI-resource authoring contract;
- #126: core MCP v2 showcase, not owner of the Apps authoring API;
- #127: turnkey community MCP Apps/Canvas template and end-to-end conformance;
- #71: native foundry vertical consuming those foundations.

No new issue was created. #125 remains unchanged. This does not create an immediately writable post-#125 lane: #127 waits for #99/#100 and the accepted post-cleanup frontend base; #126 remains separately deferred after cleanup.

Official extension: https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps/blob/10195ad91851502134930e9b80ec2c04e277a720/specification/2026-01-26/apps.mdx
Repository audit receipt: https://github.com/joshyorko/actions/issues/101#issuecomment-5320482951

## Distributed Runtime architecture refinement receipt — 2026-08-18

Stage 3 now includes the original #83–#92 work plus four missing contracts discovered through deeper Sema4.ai docs, Gallery/Cookbook, Data Package, Work Item, and Kubernetes blueprint archaeology:

- #129 — Workspace-scoped Deployments, immutable revision pinning, and provider bindings;
- #130 — Action Package v3 capability bundles and bounded exposure projections;
- #131 — open provider-based data and knowledge plane;
- #132 — durable Work Item leases, retries, schedules, review, and workflow lineage over Runs.

Existing issues were tightened so this is one roadmap rather than a competing architecture: #82, #83, #84, #85, #86, #87, #90, and #92 now share the same object boundaries.

Recommended dependency order after the current release/frontend critical path:

1. finish and merge #84/#109 as the single SQLite/PostgreSQL database foundation;
2. design #129 and #130 so the Runtime knows exactly what immutable Deployment/Package identities are pinned and scheduled;
3. implement #83 Run/Attempt fencing on the one Database abstraction;
4. implement #90 independent workers against #83/#129/#130;
5. map #85 Tasks and #92 interactions onto durable Runs;
6. implement #86/#87 provider boundaries;
7. keep #125 cleanup exact, then pursue #131 separately without proprietary compatibility;
8. implement #132 after the Run/worker ownership contract is real.

This remains incremental. It is not authorization for a giant rewrite, a Kubernetes operator, one server/pod per package, or a second database/job/revision system. Local single-binary mode remains a first-class collapsed implementation of the same semantics.
